          "title": "Statements and the structure of a program",
          "theory": "Programs are built from **statements**. A statement is a single instruction that makes the computer do something, and in C++ most statements end with a semicolon (`;`), the \"full stop\" of the language.\n\n```cpp\nint x;        // a declaration statement\nx = 5;        // an expression statement\nreturn 0;     // a jump statement\n```\n\n## Functions and `main`\n\nStatements live inside **functions**. A function is a named group of statements that run one after another, from top to bottom.\n\nEvery C++ program must contain exactly one function called `main`. When the program starts, execution begins at the first statement of `main`. When `main` finishes, the program ends.\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main()\n{\n    std::cout << \"Hello, world!\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nReading it line by line:\n\n- `#include <iostream>` pulls in the standard library's input/output tools. It is a preprocessor directive, not a statement, so it has no semicolon.\n- `int main()` starts the definition of `main`. The `int` says it hands back an integer.\n- `{` and `}` mark the start and end of the function body.\n- `std::cout << \"Hello, world!\\n\";` prints text to the screen.\n- `return 0;` gives a status code back to the operating system. `0` means \"everything went fine\".\n\n> [!note] `main` is the only function that may leave out `return 0;`. If you do, the compiler adds it for you.\n\n## Libraries\n\nA **library** is a collection of ready-made code you can reuse. The **C++ standard library** comes with every compiler and provides input/output, strings, math and much more. You use it by writing `#include` followed by the library's name.\n\n## Syntax and syntax errors\n\n**Syntax** is the set of grammar rules of the language. If you break one, for example by forgetting a semicolon, the compiler stops and reports a **syntax error** with a line number.\n\n> [!tip] Fix the first error in the list first. Later errors are often just echoes of it.",
