          "id": "1.2",
          "title": "Comments",
          "theory": "A **comment** is text written for humans. The compiler ignores it completely.\n\n## Two kinds of comment\n\n```cpp\n// A single-line comment runs to the end of the line.\nint x{ 5 };   // it can also follow some code\n\n/* A multi-line comment\n   can span as many lines\n   as you like. */\n```\n\nMulti-line comments **cannot be nested**. The first `*/` ends the comment, no matter how many `/*` came before it:\n\n```cpp\n/* outer /* inner */ this part is NOT a comment */\n```\n\nHere the words after the first `*/` are treated as code, which causes an error.\n\n## What good comments say\n\n- At the top of a file or function: *what* it does, in plain words.\n- Inside a function: *how* it works, when that is not obvious from the code.\n- On a statement: *why* it is there. Do not just repeat what the code says.\n\n```cpp\nx = x + 1;   // Bad: add one to x\nx = x + 1;   // Good: skip the header row\n```\n\nWrite comments for a reader who does not know what you were thinking, which usually means yourself in three months.\n\n## Commenting out code\n\nPutting `//` in front of a statement disables it without deleting it. This is handy for testing:\n\n```cpp\nstd::cout << \"A\";\n// std::cout << \"B\";   // never runs\nstd::cout << \"C\";      // prints: AC\n```\n\n> [!tip] Do not leave big blocks of commented-out code in finished programs. Version control remembers old code for you.",
