          "title": "Introduction to objects and variables",
          "theory": "Computers store data in **memory**. Picture memory as a huge row of numbered mailboxes: each one can hold data, and each has an **address** you could use to find it. In practice you rarely deal with addresses. You give things names instead.\n\nAn **object** is a region of memory that can hold a value. An object that has a name is a **variable**, and its name is called an **identifier**.\n\n## Defining a variable\n\n```cpp\nint x;    // define a variable named x that holds an integer\n```\n\n- `int` is the **data type**. It tells the compiler how much memory to reserve and how to interpret the bits stored there (here: a whole number).\n- `x` is the identifier.\n- When the program runs this line, memory is set aside for `x`. Creating an object like this is called **instantiation**.\n\nC++ is **statically typed**: a variable's type is fixed when it is defined and can never change.\n\n## Several variables at once\n\nVariables of the **same** type can be defined in one statement, separated by commas:\n\n```cpp\nint width, height;    // same as: int width; int height;\n```\n\nMixing types this way is an error: `int a, double b;` does not compile. Defining one variable per line is usually easier to read and to comment.\n\n## Define before you use\n\nThe compiler reads your code from top to bottom, so a variable must be defined before it is used:\n\n```cpp\nx = 4;     // error: x has not been defined yet\nint x;\n```\n\n> [!note] Defining a variable does not give it a value. The next two lessons cover giving variables values, and what goes wrong when you forget.",
