          "id": "1.4",
          "title": "Variable assignment and initialization",
          "theory": "There are two different ways to put a value into a variable, and it is important not to confuse them.\n\n```cpp\nint x;      // definition: x exists but has no value yet\nx = 5;      // assignment: store 5 in x (replacing whatever was there)\n```\n\n**Assignment** changes the value of a variable that already exists. **Initialization** gives a variable its first value *at the moment it is created*.\n\n## Four ways to initialize\n\n```cpp\nint a = 5;     // copy initialization\nint b(5);      // direct initialization\nint c{ 5 };    // direct list (brace) initialization\nint d{};       // value initialization: d becomes 0\n```\n\nAll of them start the variable off with a value. What differs is how strict they are. With braces, a **narrowing conversion**, one that could lose information, is an error:\n\n```cpp\nint e = 4.5;    // compiles, silently stores 4\nint f{ 4.5 };   // error: narrowing conversion from double to int\n```\n\nThe compiler catches the mistake for you instead of quietly throwing away the `.5`.\n\n> [!tip] Best practice: initialize every variable when you define it, and prefer braces. Use empty braces `{}` when the value is going to be replaced right away, for example before you read input into the variable.\n\n## Initialization with expressions\n\nThe starting value does not have to be a literal number. Any expression works:\n\n```cpp\nint width{ 7 };\nint height{ 3 };\nint area{ width * height };   // area starts as 21\n```\n\nLater, `area = 30;` would be an assignment, because `area` already exists.",
