          "title": "Introduction to iostream: cout, cin, and endl",
          "theory": "Input and output come from the standard library's `<iostream>` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n```\n\nIt gives you `std::cout` (output), `std::cin` (input) and `std::endl`. The `std::` prefix says these names live in the **standard namespace**.\n\n## Output with `std::cout`\n\n`std::cout` (\"character output\") sends text to the console. The **insertion operator** `<<` pushes the value on its right into the stream, and you can chain several in one statement:\n\n```cpp\nint x{ 5 };\nstd::cout << \"x is \" << x << '\\n';    // prints: x is 5\n```\n\nText in double quotes is a **string literal**; a single character goes in single quotes.\n\n### New lines\n\nUse `'\\n'` to end a line. `std::endl` also ends the line, but it additionally *flushes* the output buffer, which is slower.\n\n> [!tip] Prefer `'\\n'` over `std::endl` in most code.\n\n## Input with `std::cin`\n\n`std::cin` reads what the user types. The **extraction operator** `>>` moves the next value from the input into a variable. Values are separated by whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines), and extractions chain like insertions:\n\n```cpp\nint a{};\nint b{};\nstd::cin >> a >> b;      // input \"3 4\"  gives  a = 3, b = 4\n```\n\nThe type of the variable decides how the text is read. Reading into an `int` expects digits.\n\n## A complete example\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main()\n{\n    std::cout << \"Enter a number: \";\n    int x{};\n    std::cin >> x;\n    std::cout << \"Double: \" << x * 2 << '\\n';\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n> [!note] In this app, type the program's input into the **Input** box before pressing Run. It plays the role of the keyboard.",
