          "id": "1.6",
          "title": "Uninitialized variables and undefined behavior",
          "theory": "An **uninitialized variable** is one that has been defined but never given a value:\n\n```cpp\nint x;                // no value yet\nstd::cout << x;       // reads leftover bits: undefined behavior\n```\n\nC++ does not clear memory for you. The variable simply occupies a spot in memory that still holds whatever bits were left there before, so reading it gives an unpredictable value.\n\n## Undefined behavior\n\n**Undefined behavior (UB)** is what you get when you write code that the language gives no rules for. Anything can happen:\n\n- a random-looking value,\n- the same wrong value every run,\n- a crash,\n- or the worst case: it appears to work, and breaks later when you change something unrelated (the compiler, the operating system, an unrelated line).\n\nOther common sources of UB are dividing an integer by zero and using an index outside a string or array.\n\n> [!warn] \"It worked when I ran it\" proves nothing about code with undefined behavior. Never rely on it.\n\nIn this app, the built-in C++ interpreter stops with an error when you read an uninitialized variable, so you can see exactly where the mistake is.\n\n## How to avoid it\n\n- Initialize variables when you define them: `int x{};` or `int x{ 5 };`.\n- If you have to define a variable first, make sure it is assigned before its first read. Reading into it with `std::cin >> x;` counts as assigning it.\n\n```cpp\nint x;\nstd::cin >> x;      // fine: x gets a value before it is used\nstd::cout << x;\n```\n\n## A related idea: implementation-defined behavior\n\nSome things are decided by the compiler and platform, such as how many bytes an `int` occupies (usually 4). This is **implementation-defined**: it can differ between systems, but each compiler must stay consistent and document its choice. That makes it very different from undefined behavior.",
