          "theory": "**Keywords** are words C++ reserves for itself, such as `int`, `return`, `if`, `while` and `class`. There are about 90 of them, and you cannot use them as names.\n\nAn **identifier** is a name you choose for a variable, function or other entity.\n\n## Rules for identifiers\n\n1. Only letters (`a`-`z`, `A`-`Z`), digits and underscores. No spaces, hyphens or other symbols.\n2. It cannot start with a digit.\n3. It cannot be a keyword.\n4. It is **case sensitive**: `score`, `Score` and `SCORE` are three different names.\n\n| Name | Valid? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| `total_score` | yes | letters and underscore |\n| `userAge` | yes | letters only |\n| `2nd_place` | no | starts with a digit |\n| `my-value` | no | `-` is the minus operator |\n| `my value` | no | contains a space |\n| `class` | no | it is a keyword |\n\n## Naming conventions\n\nThese are not enforced by the compiler, but they make code far easier to read:\n\n- Start variable names with a lowercase letter.\n- Avoid names that start with an underscore; many of them are reserved for the implementation.\n- For several words, pick `snake_case` or `camelCase` and use it consistently.\n- Name a variable for what it holds. Avoid cryptic abbreviations.\n- Very short names are fine for tiny scopes (like `i` in a loop), but longer scopes deserve more descriptive names.\n\n```cpp\nint n;            // unclear\nint numApples;    // clear\n```",
