          "id": "1.8",
          "title": "Whitespace and basic formatting",
          "theory": "**Whitespace** means spaces, tabs and newlines. C++ mostly ignores it, but not entirely. It matters in these places:\n\n1. **Between tokens.** `return 0;` works but `return0;` is a single unknown name.\n2. **Inside quoted text.** Spaces inside `\"...\"` are kept exactly. A string literal cannot contain a raw line break; use `\\n` instead.\n3. **At the end of a `//` comment.** The comment stops at the newline.\n\nEverywhere else the compiler does not care. To the compiler these two programs are identical:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\nint main(){std::cout<<\"Hi\\n\";return 0;}\n```\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main()\n{\n    std::cout << \"Hi\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nOnly one of them is pleasant to read, so formatting is about helping humans.\n\n## Basic formatting guidelines\n\n- Indent the body of every `{ }` by one level (a tab or four spaces).\n- Put one statement on each line.\n- Keep lines to about 80 characters. When a statement is too long, break it and start the continuation line with the operator:\n\n```cpp\nstd::cout << \"The result is \"\n          << result\n          << '\\n';\n```\n\n- Use blank lines to separate logical groups of statements.\n- Choose a brace style and stay consistent. (This course puts a function's opening `{` on its own line.)\n- Let a formatter such as `clang-format` do the work for you.\n\n> [!tip] Adjacent string literals are joined by the compiler: `\"Hello, \" \"World\"` is the same as `\"Hello, World\"`. This lets you split long text over several lines.",
