          "id": "1.10",
          "title": "Introduction to expressions",
          "theory": "An **expression** is a combination of literals, variables, operators and function calls that can be evaluated to produce a **single value**. That value is the expression's **result**.\n\n```cpp\n2            // literal: evaluates to 2\nx            // variable: evaluates to the value stored in x\n2 + 3        // evaluates to 5\nx * 2 + 1    // evaluates to whatever x * 2 + 1 comes to\n```\n\nWhen the program runs, the compiler-generated code **evaluates** expressions to find their values. You can use an expression anywhere a value is expected:\n\n```cpp\nint x{ 2 };\nint y{ x + 3 };          // initialized with the expression x + 3 (5)\nstd::cout << y * 2;      // prints the result of y * 2 (10)\n```\n\nEven assignment is an expression: `x = 5` stores 5 in `x` and evaluates to `x`.\n\n## Expression statements\n\nAdding a semicolon to an expression turns it into an **expression statement**, which evaluates the expression and then discards the result:\n\n```cpp\nx = 5;                  // assignment, result discarded\nstd::cout << x;         // output, result discarded\n2 * 3;                  // legal but useless; compilers usually warn\n```\n\n## Side effects\n\nMany expressions do more than produce a value. A **side effect** is a lasting change beyond the result: assigning to a variable (`x = 5;`), printing (`std::cout << x;`), or reading input (`std::cin >> x;`).\n\nExpression statements exist mainly for their side effects. `2 * 3;` has none, so it does nothing useful.\n\n## Expressions versus statements\n\n| | Expression | Statement |\n|---|---|---|\n| Purpose | computes a value | performs an action |\n| Example | `x + 1` | `x = x + 1;` |\n\n> [!tip] If you can put it where a value is expected, it is an expression. A statement is a complete instruction that ends in `;`.",
          "recap": [
            "An expression combines literals, variables, operators and calls to produce one value.",
            "Variables evaluate to their stored value; assignment is also an expression.",
            "An expression statement is an expression followed by `;`; its result is discarded.",
            "A side effect is a change that lasts beyond the result (assigning, printing, reading)."
          ],
