          "title": "Developing your first program",
          "theory": "Writing a program is easier when you design it before typing it. Here is a simple process that works for small and big programs alike.\n\n## Four steps\n\n1. **Define the problem.** State in one or two sentences what the program must do. Example: \"Ask for a number of minutes and show it as hours and minutes.\"\n2. **Plan the solution.** Work out how you would solve it by hand. For minutes: hours = minutes / 60 (whole part), remainder = minutes % 60.\n3. **Write it in small steps.** Get a tiny version running, then add one piece at a time.\n4. **Test.** Run it with several inputs, including edge cases such as `0` or exact multiples.\n\n## Build it incrementally\n\nStart with only the skeleton and confirm it compiles:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main()\n{\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nAdd input, and print it back to check it was read correctly:\n\n```cpp\nint minutes{};\nstd::cin >> minutes;\nstd::cout << minutes << '\\n';   // temporary check\n```\n\nThen add the calculation:\n\n```cpp\nstd::cout << minutes / 60 << \" h \" << minutes % 60 << \" min\\n\";\n```\n\nAt each step the program works, so when something breaks you know it was the last thing you added.\n\n> [!tip] Keep each step small enough that you can predict the exact output before you run it. If the result surprises you, you have found a misunderstanding early.\n\n## Typical beginner traps\n\n- Writing everything at once, then facing a wall of errors.\n- Forgetting to test unusual inputs.\n- Not reading the problem again at the end: does the output match what was asked, character for character?",
