          "id": "2.2",
          "title": "Function return values (value-returning functions)",
          "theory": "A function can send a value back to its caller. That value is the function's **return value**, and its type is the **return type** written before the name.\n\n```cpp\nint readNumber()\n{\n    int x{};\n    std::cin >> x;\n    return x;        // hand x back to the caller\n}\n```\n\n## Using the returned value\n\nA call to a value-returning function is an expression that evaluates to the returned value:\n\n```cpp\nint main()\n{\n    int n{ readNumber() };            // initialize n with the result\n    std::cout << readNumber() * 2;    // use it inside a larger expression\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nIf the caller ignores the value (`readNumber();`) the program still runs, but the value is lost.\n\n## The `return` statement\n\n- `return expression;` ends the function immediately and sends the value back.\n- The value is converted to the declared return type.\n- Any statements after a `return` in the same path never run.\n\n> [!warn] A function with a non-`void` return type **must** return a value on every path. Reaching the closing `}` without one is undefined behavior. `main` is the one exception: it returns `0` automatically.\n\n## One value only\n\nA function can return **one** value. (Later lessons show how to get several results out.)\n\n## `main`'s return value\n\n`main` returns an `int` **status code** to the operating system. `0` means success; any other value signals a problem. You will almost always write `return 0;`.",
