          "title": "Introduction to function parameters and arguments",
          "theory": "Functions become far more useful when the caller can give them data to work with.\n\n## Parameters and arguments\n\n- A **parameter** is a variable declared in the function's parentheses.\n- An **argument** is the value the caller supplies for it.\n\n```cpp\nint add(int x, int y)      // x and y are parameters\n{\n    return x + y;\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    std::cout << add(3, 4);   // 3 and 4 are arguments\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nWhen `add(3, 4)` runs, `x` is initialized with a **copy** of `3` and `y` with a copy of `4`. This is called **pass by value**. Changing a parameter inside the function does not change the caller's variable.\n\n```cpp\nvoid tryChange(int n) { n = 99; }\n\nint a{ 5 };\ntryChange(a);     // a is still 5\n```\n\n## Rules\n\n- Parameters are separated by commas, and **each needs its own type**: `int add(int x, int y)`. Writing `int add(int x, y)` is an error.\n- The caller must pass the right number of arguments, in the same order.\n- Arguments can be any expression: `add(a, b * 2)`.\n- Parameters work like local variables that start with the argument's value.\n\n> [!note] If the argument type differs from the parameter type, the compiler converts it when it can (for example `int` to `double`). Narrowing conversions may lose information, so be careful.",
