          "title": "Introduction to local scope",
          "theory": "Variables defined inside a function are **local variables**. Two ideas describe them: *scope* and *lifetime*.\n\n## Scope: where a name can be used\n\nA local variable's **scope** runs from the line where it is defined to the closing `}` of its block. Outside that region the name does not exist.\n\n```cpp\nint main()\n{\n    int x{ 1 };         // x is in scope from here...\n    {\n        int y{ 2 };     // y is in scope only inside these braces\n    }\n    // std::cout << y;  // error: y is out of scope\n    return 0;\n}                       // ...to here\n```\n\nFunction parameters are local to their function too.\n\n## Lifetime: when the object exists\n\nA local variable is created when execution reaches its definition and destroyed when the block ends. After that its memory can be reused.\n\n## Names are per function\n\nTwo functions can use the same variable name without any conflict. They are completely separate:\n\n```cpp\nint doubleIt(int n)\n{\n    int result{ n * 2 };\n    return result;\n}\n\nint main()\n{\n    int result{ 10 };          // a different \"result\"\n    int r{ doubleIt(4) };\n    std::cout << result;       // prints 10, not 8\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n> [!tip] Define a variable as close as possible to where you first use it. Short scopes are easier to read and harder to misuse.",
