          "id": "2.6",
          "title": "Why functions are useful, and how to use them effectively",
          "theory": "Functions are the main tool for keeping programs manageable. Here is why they pay off.\n\n## Benefits\n\n- **Organization.** A long program becomes a few short, named steps. `main` can read like an outline.\n- **Reusability.** Write once, call from many places.\n- **Easier fixes.** If a rule changes, you change it in one function instead of hunting for copies.\n- **Testing.** A small function can be tested on its own with known inputs.\n- **Abstraction.** The caller only needs to know *what* a function does, not *how*.\n\n## Don't repeat yourself\n\nDuplicated code is a bug waiting to happen: fix it in one place and forget the other. If you paste the same lines twice, make them a function.\n\n```cpp\n// repeated\nstd::cout << \"Total: \" << a + b << '\\n';\nstd::cout << \"Total: \" << c + d << '\\n';\n\n// better\nvoid printTotal(int x, int y) { std::cout << \"Total: \" << x + y << '\\n'; }\nprintTotal(a, b);\nprintTotal(c, d);\n```\n\n## Writing good functions\n\n- **One job each.** If you need \"and\" to describe it, consider splitting it.\n- **A clear name**, usually a verb: `readNumber`, `printReport`, `isEven`.\n- **Short.** When a function no longer fits on a screen, look for pieces to move out.\n- **Few parameters.** Many parameters usually mean the function does too much.\n- Prefer functions that *compute and return* a value over ones that read input and print inside, because they are easier to reuse and test.",
