          "id": "2.7",
          "title": "Forward declarations and definitions",
          "theory": "The compiler reads your file from top to bottom. If you call a function **before** the compiler has seen it, you get an error:\n\n```cpp\nint main()\n{\n    std::cout << multiply(6, 7);   // error: multiply was not declared\n    return 0;\n}\n\nint multiply(int x, int y) { return x * y; }\n```\n\n## Forward declarations\n\nYou could reorder the code, but that is not always possible (two functions may call each other). The fix is a **forward declaration**: a line that tells the compiler a function exists, without giving its body.\n\n```cpp\nint multiply(int x, int y);    // declaration (also called a prototype)\n\nint main()\n{\n    std::cout << multiply(6, 7);   // fine now\n    return 0;\n}\n\nint multiply(int x, int y)     // definition\n{\n    return x * y;\n}\n```\n\nA declaration consists of the return type, name, parameter types and a **semicolon**. Parameter names are optional there: `int multiply(int, int);` works too, but keeping the names documents the code.\n\n## Declaration vs definition\n\n- A **declaration** tells the compiler a name exists and what type it has.\n- A **definition** actually creates it: the function body, or a variable's storage.\n- Every definition is also a declaration.\n\n## One Definition Rule\n\n- A function (or variable) may be **defined only once** in the program.\n- It may be **declared** as many times as you like.\n\n> [!warn] If you declare a function and call it but never define it anywhere, the compiler is happy but the **linker** fails with an \"undefined reference\" error.",
          "recap": [
            "Functions must be declared before they are called.",
            "A forward declaration is the function header followed by `;`.",
            "Declarations introduce names; definitions create them. Define once, declare as often as needed.",
            "A declared but undefined function causes a linker error."
          ],
