          "id": "2.9",
          "title": "Naming collisions and an introduction to namespaces",
          "theory": "## Naming collisions\n\nTwo different things cannot share the same name in the same scope. If they do, you get a **naming collision**:\n\n```cpp\n// a.cpp\nint value() { return 1; }\n\n// b.cpp\nint value() { return 2; }   // linker error: value is defined twice\n```\n\nIn small programs this is rare. In big ones, and when you combine code from several libraries, it becomes a real risk.\n\n## Namespaces\n\nA **namespace** is a named region that gives its contents their own scope. Names in different namespaces do not collide.\n\n```cpp\nnamespace Math\n{\n    int value() { return 1; }\n}\n\nnamespace Game\n{\n    int value() { return 2; }\n}\n```\n\n## The scope resolution operator `::`\n\nTo reach a name inside a namespace, write the namespace, `::`, then the name:\n\n```cpp\nstd::cout << Math::value();   // 1\nstd::cout << Game::value();   // 2\n```\n\nYou have already used this: `std::cout` means \"`cout`, which lives in the namespace `std`\". **`std`** holds the entire C++ standard library.\n\n## The global namespace\n\nNames declared outside any namespace belong to the **global namespace**. `main` lives there. A name with a leading `::` (like `::value`) explicitly means the global one.\n\n## `using namespace`\n\n`using namespace std;` lets you drop the `std::` prefix, but it brings *every* standard name into your scope, which can reintroduce collisions.\n\n> [!tip] Prefer writing `std::` explicitly. It is a few extra characters, and it removes any doubt about where a name comes from.",
          "recap": [
            "A naming collision happens when two entities share a name in the same scope.",
            "Namespaces create separate scopes; `::` selects a name from one.",
            "The standard library lives in `std`; non-namespaced names are in the global namespace.",
            "Avoid `using namespace std;` in real code."
          ],
          "exercises": [
            {
              "type": "choice",
              "prompt": "In `Game::value()`, what is `::` called and what does it do?",
              "options": [
                "The insertion operator; it sends data to the console",
                "The scope resolution operator; it says that `value` is found inside the namespace `Game`",
                "The assignment operator; it stores a function in a namespace",
                "A comment marker; everything after it is ignored"
              ],
              "answer": 1,
              "explain": "`::` is the scope resolution operator. `Game::value` means \"the `value` that belongs to namespace `Game`\", so it cannot collide with a `value` in another namespace."
            }
          ]
