          "id": "2.12",
          "title": "Header guards",
          "theory": "Headers can include other headers, so the same header may reach a file by two routes. If it contains a definition, the compiler sees that definition twice in one translation unit and reports a redefinition error.\n\n```cpp\n// square.h (contains a definition on purpose)\nint getSquareSides() { return 4; }\n\n// wave.h\n#include \"square.h\"\n\n// main.cpp\n#include \"square.h\"\n#include \"wave.h\"     // brings square.h in a second time: error\n```\n\n## Header guards\n\nA **header guard** makes a header include its contents only the first time. It uses conditional compilation:\n\n```cpp\n#ifndef SQUARE_H\n#define SQUARE_H\n\n// contents of the header go here\n\n#endif\n```\n\nHow it works:\n\n1. First inclusion: `SQUARE_H` is not defined, so the code runs through `#define SQUARE_H` and the contents are kept.\n2. Second inclusion: `SQUARE_H` is already defined, so everything up to `#endif` is skipped.\n\nRules for the name:\n\n- Make it **unique** to the file. A common pattern is the file name in capitals, optionally with the project name: `MYPROJECT_SQUARE_H`.\n- Two headers with the same guard name would silently hide each other.\n\n## `#pragma once`\n\nMost compilers also accept a one-line alternative:\n\n```cpp\n#pragma once\n```\n\nIt is shorter and cannot be mistyped, but it is not part of the official C++ standard. Header guards work everywhere.\n\n## What guards do not do\n\nThey stop a header from being included twice **within one file**. They do not stop it being included once in *each* of several `.cpp` files. So a function **defined** in a header still breaks the One Definition Rule across files, which is one more reason to keep definitions out of headers.",
          "recap": [
            "Including a header twice in one file can cause redefinition errors.",
            "A header guard (`#ifndef` / `#define` / `#endif`) makes later inclusions empty.",
            "Use a unique guard name per header; `#pragma once` is a common shortcut.",
            "Guards work per translation unit; they do not make header definitions safe across several source files."
          ],
