          "id": "3.4",
          "title": "Basic debugging tactics",
          "theory": "You do not need special tools to start debugging. These tactics work with just a compiler.\n\n## 1. Comment out code\n\nDisable a section with `//` or `/* */` and see whether the problem changes. If it disappears, the cause is in the part you removed. It also shrinks the area you have to think about.\n\n## 2. Check the flow\n\nMake sure the function you are debugging is actually called, and in the order you expect, by printing its name:\n\n```cpp\nint add(int x, int y)\n{\n    std::cout << \"add() called\\n\";\n    return x + y;\n}\n```\n\n## 3. Print values\n\nPrint the inputs, intermediate results and outputs, and compare them against what you expect:\n\n```cpp\nint sum{ 0 };\nfor (int i{ 1 }; i < n; ++i)\n{\n    sum += i;\n    std::cout << \"i=\" << i << \" sum=\" << sum << '\\n';   // debug\n}\n```\n\nSeeing `i` stop one short of `n` reveals the bug at once.\n\n## Tips\n\n- Give debug lines a recognizable look, such as a `DEBUG:` prefix, so they are easy to find and delete.\n- Do not indent them like real code; being visually different makes it harder to forget them.\n- Print *before* the suspicious line, as well as after. A crash can swallow output that came later.\n- **Remove or disable** debug output before you finish.\n\n## Downsides\n\n- It clutters the code and the output.\n- Each experiment means editing and recompiling.\n- You may accidentally leave a statement behind, or change the behavior while adding it.\n\nLater lessons show cleaner options, including conditional compilation and a real debugger.",
