          "title": "More debugging tactics",
          "theory": "Printing values works, but scattering `std::cout` lines through your code is messy. Here are cleaner variants.\n\n## Conditional debugging code\n\nWrap debug statements in conditional compilation, controlled by a single switch near the top of the file:\n\n```cpp\n#define ENABLE_DEBUG       // comment this line out to disable all debug code\n\n#ifdef ENABLE_DEBUG\n    std::cout << \"DEBUG: sum = \" << sum << '\\n';\n#endif\n```\n\nYou can leave the debug code in place permanently. With the `#define` removed, the preprocessor deletes it before compiling, so it costs nothing at run time and cannot change the final program's behavior.\n\n## Flush matters\n\nOutput can be **buffered**: held in memory and written later. If a program crashes, buffered text may never appear, and you will wrongly conclude the crash happened earlier than it did. Two ways around this:\n\n- `std::cerr` is the standard *error* stream. It is unbuffered, so its output appears immediately. It is a good place for debug messages.\n- `std::endl` flushes the buffer, which is the one time it is preferable to `'\\n'`.\n\n```cpp\nstd::cerr << \"DEBUG: reached step 3\\n\";\n```\n\n## Loggers\n\nA **logger** is a small tool or library that writes timestamped messages with levels (info, warning, error) to the console or a file. Large programs use one so that diagnostic output can be filtered, saved and turned on or off without editing code.",
