          "id": "3.6",
          "title": "Using an integrated debugger: Stepping",
          "theory": "A **debugger** is a program that runs your program under your control. Instead of adding print statements, you can pause execution and watch what happens. Every IDE (Visual Studio, Code::Blocks, CLion, VS Code) includes one, and the same ideas apply in all of them.\n\n## Stepping\n\n**Stepping** means running a program one statement at a time. While it is paused, an arrow or highlight, the **execution marker**, shows the statement that will run **next** (it has not run yet).\n\n## The stepping commands\n\n| Command | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Step into** | Run the next statement. If it is a function call, jump inside the function and pause on its first line. |\n| **Step over** | Run the next statement completely. A function call runs in one go and you pause on the line after it. |\n| **Step out** | Run until the current function returns, then pause in the caller. |\n\nChoosing between them:\n\n- Use **step over** for calls you trust (like `std::cout`).\n- Use **step into** for functions you want to inspect.\n- If you stepped into a function by mistake, **step out**.\n\n## Why it helps\n\nYou see the real order of execution, which branch an `if` took, and how many times a loop repeats, without changing the source code at all.\n\n> [!tip] Step into a call to a library function such as `std::cout <<` can drop you into the standard library's code. Step out, and use step over next time.",
