          "title": "Using an integrated debugger: Watching variables",
          "theory": "Pausing is only useful if you can look at the data. Debuggers let you inspect variables while the program is paused.\n\n## Seeing values\n\n- **Hover** the mouse over a variable in the editor to see its current value.\n- The **Locals** (or *Autos*) window lists the variables in the current scope together with their values. Values that just changed are usually highlighted.\n- The **Watch** window holds expressions you choose, and shows their value every time execution pauses.\n\n## Watch expressions\n\nA watch is not limited to single variables. You can enter expressions such as:\n\n```\nsum\ni * 2\ntotal / count\n```\n\nThe debugger re-evaluates them at each pause, so you can see exactly how a value evolves as you step, and notice the instant it becomes wrong.\n\n## What to look for\n\n- A value that is not what you expected, which tells you the bug is at or before this point.\n- A variable that never changes inside a loop.\n- Garbage-looking numbers (huge, negative, or strange): usually an **uninitialized variable**.\n- Variables from other scopes appearing or disappearing as you step in and out of functions.\n\n## Changing values\n\nMost debuggers also let you edit a variable's value while paused. This is handy for testing a hypothesis (\"what if `count` were 0 here?\") without editing and recompiling.\n\n> [!note] A watch on a variable that is out of scope shows an error or \"not available\". That is normal; it comes back when execution returns to a scope where the name exists.",
