          "theory": "When a program calls a function, the computer must remember where to continue once that function returns. It keeps this record on the **call stack**.\n\n## What it is\n\nThe **call stack** is a list of all the functions that are currently active (called but not yet returned). Each entry is a **stack frame**, holding that call's local variables and its return location.\n\nTake this program:\n\n```cpp\nvoid c() { /* breakpoint here */ }\nvoid b() { c(); }\nvoid a() { b(); }\nint main() { a(); return 0; }\n```\n\nWhile paused inside `c`, the call stack looks like this (most recent call on top):\n\n```\nc()\nb()\na()\nmain()\n```\n\n- `main` is at the bottom: it was called first (by the runtime).\n- Each function called adds a frame on top.\n- When a function returns, its frame is removed.\n\n## The debugger's call stack window\n\nDebuggers show this list for you. It answers \"**how did I get here?**\" Clicking a frame displays that function's code and its local variables at the point of the call. This is especially valuable when a bug appears in a helper function that is called from many places: the stack shows which caller passed the bad value.\n\n## Infinite recursion\n\nA function that keeps calling itself without stopping adds a frame each time until memory for the stack runs out. The result is a **stack overflow**, and the program crashes. The call stack window would show the same function repeated thousands of times.",
