          "title": "Finding issues before they become problems",
          "theory": "The best bug is the one that never happens. These habits catch problems early, when they are cheapest to fix.\n\n## Writing safer code\n\n- **Keep functions short** and focused, with a clear purpose. Small pieces are easy to understand and test.\n- **Initialize every variable** when you define it.\n- **Avoid unnecessary cleverness.** Straightforward code has fewer places to hide mistakes.\n- **Keep scopes small**, defining variables near their first use.\n- **Do not repeat yourself.** One copy of a rule means one place to get it right.\n- Prefer clear names over comments that explain confusing ones.\n\n## Letting tools help\n\n- **Turn on compiler warnings** (for GCC/Clang: `-Wall -Wextra -pedantic`) and fix every one. Many bugs are reported as warnings.\n- Consider **treating warnings as errors** so none are ignored.\n- **Static analysis tools** read your code without running it and flag likely mistakes.\n- Use **version control** (such as git), so you can compare against the last working version when something breaks.\n\n## Test early and often\n\n- Test each function as soon as you write it, with normal input and **edge cases** (zero, negative numbers, the largest and smallest values, empty input).\n- Re-run old tests after each change to catch regressions.\n- Keep a few small programs or test cases that reproduce past bugs.\n\n## Check your assumptions\n\nIf your code relies on something being true (a number is positive, a divisor is non-zero), verify it or handle the case explicitly. Later lessons introduce error handling and assertions, which turn silent wrong behavior into a clear error.",
